#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147333 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147397 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b19641aa-8e39-5348-8565-9f4ce030d18f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Net Change of a Function Given Its Rate of Change"}] :question/id #uuid "a3030491-423d-5633-8208-b373bfee3b06" :question/math-academy-id "e-3892" :question/problem "At a shopping mall, the rate at which people are entering the mall is given by\n\n$$\nr(t) = 300t^2+50,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in people per hour, and $t>0$ is the number of hours since the mall opened. In addition, people are leaving the mall at a steady rate of $25$ people per hour. \n\n- Calculate the number of people that entered the mall in the first two hours.\n\n- Calculate the net change in the number of people inside the mall in the first 2 hours." :question/worked-solution "**Part 1**\n\nPeople are entering the mall at a rate of $r(t) = 300t^2+50.$ To find out how many people entered the mall between $t=0$ and $t=2,$ we integrate $r(t)$ between these two limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}r(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}300{t}^{2}+50\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={100{t}^{3}+50t|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(100\\cdot {2}^{3}+50(2))-(0) \\\\  & =(800+100) \\\\  & =900\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Part 2**\n\nPeople are entering the mall at a rate of $r(t) = 300t^2+50$ people per hour, and are leaving the mall at a rate of $25$ people per hour. Therefore, the **rate of change** in the number of people inside the mall, which we'll call $R(t),$ is given by:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =r(t)-25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the **net change** in the number of people, we integrate $R(t)$ between $t=0$ and $t=2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}R(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}r(t)-25\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{{\\int }_{0}^{2}r(t)\\,\\text{d}t}_{⏟}}_{900}-{\\int }_{0}^{2}25\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =900-[25t]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =900-50 \\\\  & =850\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, over the first two hours, there was a net increase of $850$ people."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147334 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147398 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f692eea3-1a29-5067-86dd-16a56be40703" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Total Change of a Function Given Its Rate of Change"}] :question/id #uuid "d007e9ad-3fd3-5ae2-864a-ef89ea01785c" :question/math-academy-id "e-3893" :question/problem "The rate at which guests are arriving at a restaurant one evening is given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t) = 6t^2+2,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in guests per hour and $t > 0$ is the time in hours since the restaurant opened. Guests are also leaving at a steady rate of $6$ guests per hour. If there were $50$ guests in the restaurant when it opened, how many guests will be in the restaurant after $1$ hour?" :question/worked-solution "Guests are entering the restaurant at a rate of $6t^2+2$ guests per hour and are leaving at a rate of $6$ guests per hour. Therefore, the **rate of change** in the number of guests (call it $R(t)$) is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =r(t)-6 \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}+2-6 \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the **total number of guests** after $t$ hours (call it $T(t)$), we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =T(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}R(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =50+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(6{x}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that $T$ is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration. \n\nTo find out the number of guests after $1$ hour, we substitute $t=1$ into the above and evaluate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1) & =50+{\\int }_{0}^{1}(6{x}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =50+{[2{x}^{3}-4x]|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =50+[(2(1)^{3}-4(1))-(0)] \\\\  & =50-2 \\\\  & =48\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147335 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147399 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3798ec35-19ef-559f-b8f8-cb0a0a03157b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Global Minimum or Maximum of a Function Given Its Rate of Change"}] :question/id #uuid "74125c40-ecdd-5453-9c49-13567fb4621c" :question/math-academy-id "e-3894" :question/problem "In example 2, we saw that the number of guests in a restaurant is given by\n\n$$\nT(t) = 50 + \\int_0^t \\left(6x^2-4\\right)\\,\\textrm d x\\,.\n$$\n\nGiven that $T(t)$ has a local minimum for some $t>0,$ find the time when the number of guests is at its minimum value." :question/worked-solution "To find the time when the number of guests is minimized, we need to solve $\\dfrac{\\textrm d T}{\\textrm d t} = 0.$\n\nDifferentiating $T(t)$ with respect to $t$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(50+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(6{x}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(50)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(6{x}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(6{x}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving $\\dfrac{\\textrm d T}{\\textrm d t} = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n6t^2-4 = 0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad t = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{2}{3}}\n$$\n\nSo we have $2$ critical points $t=\\sqrt{\\dfrac 2 3}$ and $t=0$ (the endpoint). But since the local minimum exists for $t>0,$ $t=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2}{3}}$ must be the minimum value."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319358 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147398 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f692eea3-1a29-5067-86dd-16a56be40703" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Total Change of a Function Given Its Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dcac5d0e-f796-5fa5-9733-a3ca8c3b5eff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "260-3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318305}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dcac5d0e-f796-5fa5-9733-a3ca8c3b5eff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "260-3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318305} :answer-field/id #uuid "17091112-eedf-50be-b897-838934035785" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318474}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "894497b2-7451-5370-bf78-0958e2308d18" :question/math-academy-id "q-349983" :question/problem "A zoo is tracking the number of penguins in a habitat.\nThey observe that new penguins arrive at a rate $r(t)$ given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=30{e}^{-t/2},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in penguins per month, and $t>0$ is the time in months since the tracking began.\nAt the same time, $3$ penguins are transferred out each month.\n\nIf there were initially $200$ penguins in the habitat, find the expression that gives $P(t),$ the number of penguins in the habitat $t$ months after the tracking began.\n\n$P(t)=-60{e}^{-t/2}\\,+$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The number of penguins is increasing at a rate of $r(t)$ penguins per month and is decreasing at a rate of $3$ penguins per month. Therefore, the **rate of change** $R(t)$ of the number of penguins is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =r(t)-3 \\\\  & =30{e}^{-t/2}-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the total number $P(t)$ of penguins after $t$ months, we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(t) & =P(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}R(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =200+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(30{e}^{-x/2}-3)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that our final function is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration.\n\nWe can simplify the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(t) & =200+{\\int }_{0}^{t}30{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{t}3\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =200+(-60{e}^{-x/2}){|}_{0}^{t}-3x{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =200+(60-60{e}^{-t/2})-3t \\\\  & =-60{e}^{-t/2}+260-3t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319361 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147399 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3798ec35-19ef-559f-b8f8-cb0a0a03157b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Global Minimum or Maximum of a Function Given Its Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5dedb264-eb4b-554b-b1fc-c33940aacd59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317884}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5dedb264-eb4b-554b-b1fc-c33940aacd59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317884} :answer-field/id #uuid "014eb3f4-5b0d-5bdd-b80d-f837749852b9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318423} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf14b68b-61e2-566a-924c-53e4c88d9ee2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318250}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf14b68b-61e2-566a-924c-53e4c88d9ee2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318250} :answer-field/id #uuid "6858d1c2-03bc-54aa-aad0-5ca01fbf3806" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318648}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "799a9b42-4f6e-5c6d-8236-7829c2135c09" :question/math-academy-id "q-350001" :question/problem "The value $P(t)$ of a painting is given by the function\n\n$$\nP(t)=250+{\\int }_{0}^{t}({3}^{x/4}-27)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ is measured in dollars, and $t>0$ is the time (in years) since the painting was created. \n\nAt what time is the value a minimum, and how much does the painting cost at this time, rounded to the nearest dollar?\n\nThe minimum value is\n$$$\n{{field-1}}\nafter\n{{field-2}}\nyears." :question/worked-solution "To find when the value is minimized, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(250+{\\int }_{0}^{t}({3}^{x/4}-27)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(250)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({\\int }_{0}^{t}({3}^{x/4}-27)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}({3}^{x/4}-27)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={3}^{t/4}-27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{t/4}-27 & =0 \\\\ {3}^{t/4} & ={3}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{t}{4} & =3 \\\\ t & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=12.$ To find out the value of the painting when $t=12,$ we figure out $P(12):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(12) & =250+{\\int }_{0}^{12}({3}^{x/4}-27)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =250+{[\\frac{4\\cdot {3}^{x/4}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-27x]|}_{0}^{12} \\\\  & =250+[(\\frac{4\\cdot 27}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-324)-(\\frac{4\\cdot 1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3})] \\\\  & =250-324+\\frac{108-4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =-74+\\frac{104}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & ≈21,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nSince $21<250,$ we conclude that the minimum value is $$\\,21$, and this happens after $12$ years."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357474 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147399 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3798ec35-19ef-559f-b8f8-cb0a0a03157b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Global Minimum or Maximum of a Function Given Its Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "469565fb-6d27-567b-b115-17fe2967823a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The minimum is $230$ fish after $5$ weeks" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355288} {:answer/id #uuid "90fa05bc-36a8-547f-aaf2-47eabb226e40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The minimum is $230$ fish after $1$ week" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355945} {:answer/id #uuid "a058f107-94e7-532a-8e45-31294473dddc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The minimum is $214$ fish after $3$ weeks" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356086} {:answer/id #uuid "d342dcb4-5a95-5454-8d7c-b07c177b7c00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The minimum is $215$ fish after $4$ weeks" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356509} {:answer/id #uuid "e59883a6-26da-5b2d-a2ad-67a91b6e4325" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The minimum is $218$ fish after $2$ weeks" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356667}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a058f107-94e7-532a-8e45-31294473dddc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The minimum is $214$ fish after $3$ weeks" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356086} :answer-field/id #uuid "c819c218-6e65-5a0e-be39-91f469445031" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357278}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2f28ae95-03b9-593b-a6e6-a24580ae5aa5" :question/math-academy-id "q-90756" :question/problem "A man stocked a pond with $250$ fish. After $t$ weeks, the number of fish in the pond is given by the function \n\n$$\nF(t)=250+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(8x-24)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time (in weeks) since the man put the fish in the pond.\n\nAt what time is the number of fish in the pond at a minimum, and how many fish are there at that time?" :question/worked-solution "To find when the number of fish in the pond is at minimum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(250+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(8x-24)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(250)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({\\int }_{0}^{t}(8x-24)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(8x-24)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8t-24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=0$:\n\n$$\n8t-24=0\\,⟹\\,t=3\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=3.$ To find out how many fish are when $t=3,$ we figure out $F(3)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(3) & =250+{\\int }_{0}^{3}(8x-24)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =250{[4{x}^{2}-24x]|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =250+[(4(3)^{2}-24(3))-(0)] \\\\  & =250-36 \\\\  & =214\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $214<250$, we conclude that the minimum number of fish in the pond is $214$, and this will happen after $3$ weeks."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357475 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147399 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3798ec35-19ef-559f-b8f8-cb0a0a03157b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Global Minimum or Maximum of a Function Given Its Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "797df782-68e4-5946-a5d3-719aacd38fd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The minimum is $150$ people after $5$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355726} {:answer/id #uuid "8b39583c-7632-5314-aab0-b5c8f4813e75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The minimum is $200$ people after $2$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355896} {:answer/id #uuid "944fd6a9-bc5d-51fc-ae21-9b1361fccffd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The minimum is $200$ people after $4$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355970} {:answer/id #uuid "9a1e95e1-91bb-5808-97fa-1c6f520895f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The minimum is $100$ people after $3$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356033} {:answer/id #uuid "9c5a5011-dcd0-5823-8c28-0c3a166590aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The minimum is $500$ people after $1$ hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356053}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a1e95e1-91bb-5808-97fa-1c6f520895f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The minimum is $100$ people after $3$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356033} :answer-field/id #uuid "715ea109-f7b4-5a47-ad9e-f36f04af9b6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357118}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0121e26f-4108-56d0-ac26-f4c71b23dab4" :question/math-academy-id "q-90758" :question/problem "The total number of people $V(t)$ who have visited a certain shopping center after $t$ hours is given by the function\n\n$$\nV(t)=1\\,000+100{\\int }_{0}^{t}(2x-6)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time (in hours) since the shopping center opened its doors to the public.\n\nAt what time is the number of people visiting the shopping center at a minimum, and how many people are in the shopping center at that time?" :question/worked-solution "To find when the number of people is at minimum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1\\,000+100{\\int }_{0}^{t}(2x-6)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1\\,000)+100\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({\\int }_{0}^{t}(2x-6)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =100\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(2x-6)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =100(2t-6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=0$:\n\n$$\n100(2t-6)=0\\,⟹\\,t=3\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=3.$ To find out how many people are in the shopping center when $t=3,$ we figure out $V(3)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(3) & =1\\,000+100{\\int }_{0}^{3}(2x-6)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1\\,000+100{[{x}^{2}-6x]|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =1\\,000+100[({3}^{2}-6(3))-(0)] \\\\  & =1\\,000-900 \\\\  & =100\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $100<1\\,000$, we conclude that the minimum number of people is $100$, and this will happen after $3$ hours."}]}
